{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ev9rhiMwDHUbuFXunDvUNV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development. It derives formal specs from C source code of Unix utilities, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - Specifications, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current State**: All PRDs and use cases are written across ten releases, test suites are complete, but no implementation has started yet—the specification phase is essentially done.\n\n**Roadmap**: Release 01.0 is in progress with `ts` ready for implementation, followed by `wc` and `cat` in 01.1, then `ls` and `du` in later releases.\n\nThe first incomplete release is rel01.0, which needs the differential testing harness and the `ts` utility. Since `ts` only depends on the test utilities, I should prioritize building the testing framework first, then move to the `ts` implementation itself.\n\nFor the initial task, I'm implementing pkg/testutils—the core differential testing harness. Looking at the requirements, I need to handle the test case data structure, binary execution, comparison logic, normalization hooks, and file-state verification. The main concern is organizing these into cohesive modules, likely starting with the DiffTest struct and related functions in a primary file. I'm counting up the sub-items across all five requirement groups—that's about 20 total—which maps well to the 5-8 requirements a single task should target. Now I'm thinking through the file structure and what should go into pkg/testutils/difftest.go, including the DiffTest struct, Normalize type, RunDiffTests function, comparison logic, and the TimestampNormalizer hook. primary type is DiffTest. So the main file is `difftest.go`. But there's also the TimestampNormalizer which is a separate concern. However, keeping it simple and within the line budget, one file should suffice.\n\nWait, let me re-read P7: \"Go packages must name the primary source file after the primary exported type, not the package name. `testutils/testutils.go` is forbidden; if the primary type is `DiffTest`, the file is `difftest.go`.\"\n\nSo the main file is `pkg/testutils/difftest.go`.\n\nFor the normalization hooks (TimestampNormalizer), I could put them in the same file or a separate file. Given the line budget of 250-350, putting everything in one file should work.\n\nNow I'm mapping out what needs to be implemented: the DiffTest struct with its fields, the Normalize function type, RunDiffTests function, binary execution and comparison logic, reporting, TimestampNormalizer, and file-state comparison. This should fit comfortably within the 200-350 line budget. Let me anchor the implementation to the PRD requirements—defining DiffTest with Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode, Normalize fields and FileExpectation for file-state comparison, then implementing RunDiffTests to accept testing.T, the Go binary path, reference binary path, and a slice of DiffTest cases. Now I'm mapping out the acceptance criteria that validate each requirement—eight total that cover the struct definition, binary execution, output handling, normalization, file comparison, timeout behavior, and code quality. I'm also identifying the key design decisions rooted in the PRD, starting with the primary source file location. primary type is DiffTest)\nD2: RunDiffTests uses t.Run for named subtests (prd001 R3.6)\nD3: Default timeout is 10 seconds, configurable via DiffTest field (prd001 R2.3)\nD4: Normalize is func([]byte) []byte; composition is a slice applied in order (prd001 R1.4, R4.3)\nD5: Working directory defaults to t.TempDir() when not specified (prd001 R2.5)\n\nThat's 5 design decisions, within the 3-5 range. But the constraint is 1 task max, so I need to scope this as a single, well-defined task. The entire pkg/testutils package implementation covers all requirements R1-R5 from prd001 and should be around 250-350 lines of Go code. Since every other utility depends on this for verification, it's the clear priority for the first release. I'll consolidate everything into one file covering the DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests function, TimestampNormalizer, and file-state comparison logic. Reporting and divergence handling will take about 30 lines, plus another 30 for the TimestampNormalizer and 15 for composition logic, with file-state comparison and helpers filling out the remaining ~50 lines—bringing the total to around 305 lines, which fits the budget. I'm leaning toward splitting this into two files following Go conventions: `difftest.go` for the core DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests function, and `normalize.go` for the Normalize type and normalizer implementations, since that keeps concerns cleanly separated. I'm going back and forth on whether to split this across multiple files or keep everything together. The Normalize type is pretty tightly integrated with DiffTest, so it probably makes sense to put them in the same file rather than separating them out. Let me just go with putting everything in `difftest.go` for now—if the stitch agent wants to reorganize later, it can.\n\nI should also check whether `pkg/testutils/` already exists in the project or if the agent will need to create it from scratch. Based on the architecture, it's defined but might not have any actual files yet, so the agent will likely need to set that up.\n\nLet me lock in the approach and move forward with the task definition.\n\nActually, I need to be more careful about mapping PRD requirements to task requirements. The constraint says each PRD requirement should map to exactly one task requirement, and I need to stay within the 5-8 requirement range. Looking at the five requirement groups, I can map each one directly: R1 through R5 each become a single task requirement, which gives me exactly 5 requirements—right in the target range.\n\nI could split R2 into binary invocation and timeout handling, or break R3 into comparison and reporting, but the constraint explicitly warns against inflating counts by splitting single requirements into sub-bullets. So 5 is the right number.\n\nNow I'm checking the acceptance criteria mapping. The PRD has 6 acceptance criteria that I need to align with the task requirements, making sure each one maps appropriately to the corresponding task requirement.\n\nI'm also keeping in mind that I shouldn't include test files in the task itself—implementation only—but the acceptance criteria still need to be verifiable through compilation, linting, and other checks that don't require separate test tasks. I need to add go.mod to the required reading since the stitch agent needs the module path. Looking at the key design decisions from the PRD: the DiffTest struct needs Args, Stdin, Env, and ExitCode fields plus a Normalize function type, binary execution should have a 10-second timeout, subtests use t.Run for naming, TimestampNormalizer handles timestamp normalization, normalizers compose as a slice, and FileExpectation handles file-state comparison. I've got my five design decisions locked in and they're all grounded in the PRD requirements. Now I'm verifying the task title format includes the requirement IDs as specified, and making sure each acceptance criterion is objectively verifiable rather than subjective. Let me finalize the output.\n\nActually, I should reconsider the file structure—splitting the implementation across two files would give better separation of concerns. I'll put the core DiffTest struct and test execution logic in one file, then move the normalization logic into a separate file for cleaner organization.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific deliverable with the files I need to create and what each one should contain.\n\nFor the difftest file, I need to define the DiffTest struct with all its fields—Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode, Normalize, WorkDir, and FileExpectations—plus the Normalize function type, and then implement RunDiffTests to execute both binaries and compare their outputs. The normalize file will handle the normalizer functions and composition logic.\n\nLooking at the requirements, R1 specifies the exact structure and types needed, while R2 describes how RunDiffTests should iterate through tests and invoke both binaries for comparison. with identical args, stdin, and environment. Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code independently from each binary. Impose a configurable timeout (default 10 seconds) on each invocation; fail the test if either binary exceeds the timeout. When WorkDir is empty, run both binaries in a per-test temporary directory created via t.TempDir(). Produce no output on passing tests. (prd001 R2.1-R2.5, R3.6)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement comparison logic: apply each Normalize function in order to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison. Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte; any difference is a test failure. Compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte; any difference is a test failure. Compare exit codes exactly; a difference is a test failure. (prd001 R3.1-R3.4)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"On test failure, report the DiffTest args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), both binaries' stdout and stderr outputs, and their exit codes. (prd001 R3.5)\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Build a TimestampNormalizer that swaps common timestamp patterns like 'Feb 19 12:34:56' or elapsed time formats with a fixed 'TIMESTAMP' placeholder. Support normalizer composition where an empty Normalize slice applies no transformations, but each function in the slice runs sequentially. (prd001 R4.1-R4.3)\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement file-state comparison: D\n\nNow I need to rewrite the next thinking chunk. The next thinking is about file-state comparison with FileExpectations. Let me write this naturally:\n\nFileExpectations lets DiffTest specify expected file paths and their content after both binaries finish running. I'll compare the actual file content byte-for-byte against expectations and report any divergence using the same format as stdout divergence. The acceptance criteria cover the build and vet checks, the DiffTest struct fields, the RunDiffTests function signature, a TimestampNormalizer for handling time-based output variations, and timeout handling with clear failure messages when binaries exceed the time limit.\n\nI'm also checking that file comparisons work byte-for-byte like stdout comparisons do. Looking at my acceptance criteria count—I have 7 after removing the note about test files being separate—which fits the 5-8 constraint. My 6 requirements are within the 3-5 range, and I've got 5 design decisions, so everything's balanced.\n\nLet me estimate the implementation size: the core structures and helpers should come to around 285 lines total, which sits comfortably in the 250-350 target. Now I need to make sure the task description is self-contained for the stitch agent, since they'll only see the task and the execution constitution—they need to know the package location, what types to define, and how everything fits together.\n\nThe required_reading section pointing to the PRD and go.mod should give them all the context they need, and the requirements section has enough specifics to implement without ambiguity. I don't think I need to include the engineering docs since those are about spec extraction, not relevant here. And since pkg/testutils/ is new with no existing code, I just need to be clear about what needs to be created from scratch.\n\nLet me finalize the task title and YAML structure now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71783,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71783},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f335501-fcaf-45fb-87b3-a5cb289a78cd","uuid":"161b1d47-12be-4364-b1c0-40bc14277900"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ev9rhiMwDHUbuFXunDvUNV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that derives formal specs from C/Perl source code of Unix utilities, generates Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies correctness through differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go packages), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets), `docs/` (specifications).\n\n**Current state**: All specification artifacts are complete or in progress (10 PRDs, 7 use cases, 4 test suites). **No Go code has been implemented yet.** The project is at the transition point from specification to synthesis.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) — single use case `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (timestamp stdin lines via ts). The ts utility depends on `pkg/testutils` for verification. Neither `pkg/testutils` nor `cmd/ts` has any implementation code.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nrel01.0 requires two components: `pkg/testutils` (the differential testing harness) and `cmd/ts` (the ts utility). Per the dependency map, ts depends on pkg/testutils but not on pkg/sys or pkg/format. The canonical ordering (libraries before consumers, P8) dictates that pkg/testutils must be implemented first.\n\nThe single task to propose is the implementation of `pkg/testutils` — the foundation that every utility in the project depends on for verification.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (complete PRD for this component -- all requirements and acceptance criteria)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (project structure, component descriptions, technology choices)\n      - go.mod (module path for import statements)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: DiffTest struct, FileExpectation struct, RunDiffTests function, binary execution, comparison, reporting, file-state comparison\n      - path: pkg/testutils/normalize.go\n        action: create\n        note: Normalize function type, TimestampNormalizer, normalizer composition helpers\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define DiffTest struct with fields: Args ([]string), Stdin ([]byte, nil means no stdin/immediate EOF), Env ([]string, nil means inherit test process environment), ExitCode (int, expected exit code), Normalize ([]Normalize, applied in order before comparison), WorkDir (string, empty means use per-test temp dir), and FileExpectations ([]FileExpectation for file-state comparison). Define Normalize as func([]byte) []byte. Define FileExpectation struct with Path (string) and Content ([]byte). (prd001 R1.1-R1.4, R5.1)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that iterates the test slice and runs each as a named subtest via t.Run. For each test, invoke both binaries with identical args, stdin, and environment. Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code independently from each binary. Impose a configurable timeout (default 10 seconds) on each invocation; fail the test if either binary exceeds the timeout with a clear timeout message. When WorkDir is empty, run both binaries in a per-test temporary directory created via t.TempDir(). Produce no output to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing test run. (prd001 R2.1-R2.5, R3.6)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement comparison logic: apply each Normalize function in slice order to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison. Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte -- any difference is a test failure. Compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte -- any difference is a test failure. Compare exit codes exactly -- any difference is a test failure. (prd001 R3.1-R3.4)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement divergence reporting: on any comparison failure, report the DiffTest args, stdin content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), reference binary stdout, Go binary stdout, reference stderr, Go stderr, and both exit codes via t.Errorf. (prd001 R3.5)\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement TimestampNormalizer as an exported Normalize function that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with the fixed placeholder string 'TIMESTAMP'. Must handle at minimum: syslog format ('Feb 19 12:34:56'), ISO-8601 datetime, and HH:MM:SS elapsed time patterns. Implement normalizer composition -- when the Normalize slice is nil or empty, no normalization is applied; otherwise each function is applied in sequence. (prd001 R4.1-R4.3)\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement file-state comparison: when DiffTest.FileExpectations is non-empty, after both binaries exit, read each expected file path relative to the working directory and compare content byte-for-byte against FileExpectation.Content. Report divergence in the same format as stdout divergence (path, expected content, actual content). (prd001 R5.1-R5.2)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Primary source file is difftest.go because the primary exported type is DiffTest. Normalizer logic goes in normalize.go. Package name is testutils. (project convention: name source file after primary exported type, not package name)\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"RunDiffTests runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run so individual test case failures are identifiable in go test output (prd001 R3.6). The subtest name is derived from the test index or a Name field if present on DiffTest.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Default timeout is 10 seconds per prd001 R2.3. Use context.WithTimeout for each binary invocation via exec.CommandContext. DiffTest may include a Timeout field (time.Duration) to override the default; when zero, the 10-second default applies.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Normalize is func([]byte) []byte. DiffTest.Normalize is a slice of Normalize functions applied in order (prd001 R4.3). When nil or empty, no normalization is applied. This supports per-test normalization and composition.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"When DiffTest.WorkDir is empty, both binaries run in a per-test temporary directory created via t.TempDir() (prd001 R2.5). This ensures test isolation and automatic cleanup.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/testutils/... compiles with no errors.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go vet ./pkg/testutils/... reports no issues.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"DiffTest struct has all fields specified in prd001 R1.1-R1.4 and R5.1: Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode, Normalize (slice of Normalize), WorkDir, FileExpectations (slice of FileExpectation).\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"RunDiffTests signature is RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) and runs each test as a named subtest.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer is an exported variable or function of type Normalize that replaces strftime-formatted timestamps with the fixed placeholder 'TIMESTAMP'.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"Binary execution uses context.WithTimeout with a 10-second default. If either binary exceeds the timeout, the test fails with a message containing the word 'timeout'.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"File-state comparison reads files from FileExpectations after binary execution and reports divergence with the file path, expected content, and actual content.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71783,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71783},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f335501-fcaf-45fb-87b3-a5cb289a78cd","uuid":"452aa57a-e5ba-4f3c-ba00-5367c3e48949"}
